virglrenderer: Venus buffer binds stay inside their memory

What: vkBindBufferMemory and vkBindBufferMemory2 check each bind before the
Mac's driver gets it: not a sparse buffer, a memory type the buffer takes, an
aligned offset, and the buffer ends inside the memory (no overflow). vkr keeps
each buffer's create flags and memory requirements from one query at create (as
#623 does for images); image and buffer binds share the rule
(vkr_device_memory_bind_ok). A refused bind ends the app's context: the guest
binds without waiting. Memory made from a resource (VkImportMemoryResourceInfoMESA)
may be no larger than the memory behind the resource, to the end of its host page
(vkr_resource keeps that size when device memory is exported): a larger one is
refused with VK_ERROR_INVALID_EXTERNAL_HANDLE, the context lives.
Why: KosmicKrisp and MoltenVK return VK_SUCCESS for a buffer bound half past its
memory's end, at an offset past it or one that wraps, unaligned, to memory smaller
than the buffer, and (MoltenVK) a 1 TiB buffer to 64 KiB: the GPU then reads and
writes outside the memory (a GPU fault resets the GPU). MoltenVK ends the process
on a memory type the buffer cannot take (Metal: "Invalid storageMode 3"). Both
also make memory from a 64 KiB resource with 1024 times its size and bind a
buffer of that size to it, past every range check (M4, macOS 27).
Upstream: OmacVM policy (the spec makes these the app's job), as #623's image
binds.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-robust.c: the b- cases bind before the patch and
end only the context after it; i-oversize is refused; binds in range, through
vkBindBufferMemory2, a Vulkan window buffer's IOSurface (KosmicKrisp,
OMACVM_VK_WINDOWS=iosurface) and memory of a resource's own size still bind;
--time: the requirements query per vkCreateBuffer.

